    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a very intense comparison. I'm a bit confused about the part where you say the p-values are \"a lie\" because of the heteroscedasticity. Could you explain the actual mathematical relationship between the variance of the residuals and the precision of the standard error? I'm trying to understand the technical mechanics of how the error term distorts the estimation, rather than the metaphor.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The mathematical relationship hinges on the fact that OLS standard errors are calculated assuming the variance of the error term ($\\sigma^2$) is constant across all observations. When heteroscedasticity is present—meaning the variance changes based on the predicted values—the formula for the standard error of the coefficients becomes biased. Specifically, if the variance of the errors is larger for certain groups of data points (often those with higher predicted values), the OLS estimator effectively \"down-weights\" the information from those high-variance points or misinterprets the spread of the data as tighter than it actually is.\n\nTechnically, the variance-covariance matrix of the OLS estimator is given by $\\sigma^2 (X'X)^{-1}$, but under heteroscedasticity, the true variance is actually $V = \\Omega (X'X)^{-1}$, where $\\Omega$ is the diagonal matrix of the individual error variances. Because OLS ignores $\\Omega$ and assumes it's a scalar multiple of the identity matrix, the resulting standard errors are inconsistent. If the errors are systematically larger where the model predicts higher values, the OLS standard errors will likely be too small, making the t-statistics",
      "source": "target",
